"""Original personal-health drill; first option is correct."""
RAW = '''
The WHO constitutional definition describes health as|physical, mental and social wellbeing, not merely absence of disease|absence of infection alone|physical fitness alone|a single laboratory result|The WHO definition includes physical, mental and social wellbeing.
Belonging and supportive relationships are most directly associated with|social wellbeing|bone density|pulse rate|joint range|Relationships and belonging are aspects of social wellbeing.
Managing emotions and seeking support are most directly associated with|mental wellbeing|a throwing technique|a bone classification|a tournament draw|Emotional coping and seeking support concern mental wellbeing.
Which statement about health dimensions is sound?|They interact and can change over time|They are completely independent|Social wellbeing is always determined by fitness|A single symptom measures all dimensions|Physical, mental and social aspects can interact and vary over time.
A person living with a chronic condition|can still experience wellbeing and participate in community life|cannot experience wellbeing in any dimension|must be excluded from all activities|can be assessed completely from appearance|A condition does not eliminate the possibility of wellbeing and meaningful participation.
Personal hygiene refers to|practices that help keep the body clean and reduce certain risks|a guarantee of lifelong immunity|a complete medical diagnosis|competition rules|Hygiene includes practices supporting cleanliness and reduction of certain risks.
Before preparing food, a useful hygiene practice is to|wash hands with soap and clean running water|use the same unwashed hands after the toilet|ignore visible dirt|assume food handling cannot transfer germs|Handwashing before food preparation helps reduce transfer of contamination.
After using the toilet, hands should ideally be washed with|soap and clean running water|water previously used for bathing|a dry cloth alone|no water or soap|Soap and clean running water are preferred for handwashing.
When soap and water are unavailable, an alternative hand rub should contain at least|60% alcohol|6% alcohol|16% alcohol|40% alcohol|CDC guidance recommends an alcohol-based hand sanitizer containing at least 60% alcohol when soap and water are unavailable.
Hand sanitizer may be less effective when hands are|visibly dirty or greasy|clean and dry before use|rubbed until dry|covered completely with sanitizer|Visible dirt and grease can reduce sanitizer effectiveness; soap and water are preferable when available.
A useful handwashing action is to|rub all hand surfaces with soap before rinsing|rinse only fingertips without soap|wash only one hand|dry hands before applying soap|Rubbing all surfaces with soap helps remove contaminants.
Personal hygiene measures|reduce some risks but cannot guarantee freedom from illness|prevent every illness in every case|replace all vaccination and healthcare|prove a person has no infection|Hygiene can reduce risk but is not an absolute guarantee.
Oral care commonly includes|brushing with fluoride toothpaste and obtaining dental advice when needed|avoiding all tooth cleaning|using any unverified remedy instead of assessment|assuming tooth pain never needs attention|Brushing with fluoride toothpaste and appropriate dental care support oral health.
Good menstrual hygiene requires attention to|clean materials, privacy and safe disposal|shame and exclusion|identical facilities in every setting regardless of access|avoiding all discussion or support|Materials, privacy, water and safe disposal support menstrual hygiene.
A school teaches handwashing but supplies no water. Which barrier remains?|lack of access to a necessary resource|excessive availability of facilities|automatic immunity to infection|a complete health assessment|Knowledge alone does not provide the water needed for the practice.
Health behaviour can be influenced by|income, housing, education and access to services|motivation alone in every case|a person's name alone|one test result alone|Social and environmental factors interact with individual choices.
Which is an environmental influence on health?|availability of safe water|a single inherited gene only|a game score|a person's initials|Safe water is part of the environment affecting health.
An inherited factor is best described as|a characteristic passed through biological inheritance|a facility built by a school|a local water supply|a handwashing station|Inherited factors are transmitted biologically, unlike environmental resources.
A risk factor is|associated with a change in the probability of an outcome|proof that every exposed person will become ill|a complete individual diagnosis|a guarantee that other factors do not matter|A risk factor relates to probability and does not make an outcome certain.
A protective factor may|reduce risk or support wellbeing|guarantee that illness is impossible|eliminate all other influences|serve as a complete diagnosis|Protective factors can help without providing absolute guarantees.
A study finds an association between a factor and an outcome. This alone|does not prove the cause of every individual case|proves every individual case has that cause|rules out alternative explanations|shows that no further evidence is needed|Association alone does not establish causation in every case.
Health promotion can include|education together with changes that improve access|information without any consideration of barriers|only treatment after illness|only a competition score|Health promotion can combine learning with supportive environments and services.
Which intervention addresses both knowledge and access?|teach handwashing and maintain a soap-and-water station|teach handwashing but remove the station|install a station but prohibit its use|assume every learner has identical resources|Teaching plus maintained facilities supports practical application.
A trustworthy health claim should be evaluated for|source, evidence, date and relevance|confidence of wording alone|number of shares alone|attractive packaging alone|Source quality, evidence, currency and relevance all matter.
An advertisement promises one product cures every disease. The best response is to|check reliable evidence and seek appropriate advice|stop prescribed treatment immediately|assume the claim is true because it is confident|share it without verification|Universal cure claims are warning signs and need careful evaluation.
A study guide should be used as|educational information, not a replacement for individual assessment|a definitive diagnosis for any symptom|a reason to ignore severe symptoms|a replacement for emergency services|Educational materials cannot replace individual clinical care.
Persistent or concerning symptoms should be|discussed with an appropriately qualified professional|ignored in every case|diagnosed solely from a diagram|treated by copying an unrelated person's prescription|Persistent or concerning symptoms need appropriate individual assessment.
Severe symptoms may require|urgent help according to local emergency procedures|waiting indefinitely without assessment|an unverified online cure|a sports score|Serious symptoms can require urgent assistance.
Respecting privacy in health matters helps|protect dignity and support trust|guarantee that no illness occurs|replace informed consent|eliminate the need for clinical care|Privacy and respectful communication can support trust and dignity.
Stigma about illness can|discourage people from seeking support|guarantee access to treatment|replace accurate information|eliminate social barriers|Stigma can create barriers to care and support.
Which statement about appearance and health is most accurate?|Appearance alone is not a complete health assessment|Appearance always identifies every condition|Body size alone gives a complete diagnosis|A person's clothing determines health status|Health cannot be comprehensively determined by appearance alone.
A protective school environment could include|accessible water, soap and safe sanitation|facilities that pupils are forbidden to use|unclean water presented as safe|no maintenance of toilets|Accessible and maintained facilities support healthy practices.
Which example is primarily a physical aspect of health?|body function and physical condition|relationships and belonging only|social support alone|a person's membership in a club|Physical health concerns body condition and functioning.
Which example is primarily a social aspect of health?|supportive relationships|a pulse count|the name of a bone|blood-clotting function|Supportive relationships are a social aspect of wellbeing.
Which example is primarily related to mental wellbeing?|coping with stress and seeking support|the location of the tibia|the size of a relay zone|the definition of an artery|Coping and support-seeking relate to mental wellbeing.
An individual health decision may be constrained by|cost and availability of care|knowledge alone in every situation|a match result|a fixed rule that resources never matter|Access and cost can constrain individual choices.
A community health message is stronger when it|acknowledges barriers and offers practical support|blames everyone who lacks a resource|promises that one action prevents all disease|ignores the audience's circumstances|Practical support and attention to barriers make a message more useful and equitable.
When reviewing a health source, an older publication|may need checking against current guidance|is always current by definition|always applies unchanged to every individual|proves a claim without evidence|Guidance can change, so check currency and applicability.
Which is a reasonable response to uncertainty about a treatment?|consult an appropriately qualified professional|change the treatment solely on an anonymous claim|assume all advice applies identically to everyone|avoid asking questions|A qualified professional can help assess the individual's circumstances.
The relationship between knowledge and opportunity is best described as|both can influence whether healthy practices are possible|knowledge automatically removes every barrier|facilities are irrelevant to all behaviour|access replaces the need for accurate information|Both information and practical opportunity affect the ability to act.
'''
ITEMS = []
for line in RAW.strip().splitlines():
    stem, correct, wrong1, wrong2, wrong3, explanation = line.split('|')
    ITEMS.append((stem, (correct, wrong1, wrong2, wrong3), explanation))
